L9-A (rich): per-guest-function-ENTRY trace via the x64 JIT prolog.

Upgrades the L9-A golden oracle from the GuestFunction::Call C++ hook (which only
fires on host->guest re-entries, ~5 distinct PCs) to a hook emitted at the prolog
of EVERY JIT'd guest function, so guest->guest `bl` calls -- which jump directly
between JIT'd functions and never re-enter through GuestFunction::Call -- are now
captured too. Captured a Superman Returns boot with 2223 distinct guest PCs across
all subsystems (vs ~5 before), same on-disk XENTRC01 / 32-byte format.

HOW IT WORKS
  - A file-local host trampoline XeTraceJitProlog(void* raw_context, uint64_t
    guest_pc) reads lr / r3 / r1 / thread_id from the PPCContext (host-native /
    already byte-swapped, the SAME values GuestFunction::Call samples) and calls
    GuestTracer::EmitFuncEntry(). guest_pc is passed in (it is a compile-time
    constant per function), so it is always correct regardless of register state.
  - In X64Emitter::Emit(builder, func_info), right after the existing
    kDebugInfoTraceFunctions prolog block (stack already set up & 16B aligned),
    we emit a gated `CallNative(&XeTraceJitProlog, current_guest_function_)`.
    CallNative routes through the standard guest->host thunk (CallNativeSafe),
    which saves/restores volatile regs + MXCSR and passes the PPCContext as the
    host fn's first arg, so it is fully ABI-safe and does not perturb guest state.
  - The EMISSION is gated on GuestTracer::enabled() (the same XENIA_GUEST_TRACE
    env, checked once at JIT time), so with tracing OFF the JIT'd code is
    byte-for-byte identical to stock Xenia and pays zero per-call overhead.

ON-DISK FORMAT IS UNCHANGED: same xenia_trace.h (XENTRC01 + 32-byte records), so
scripts/l9/divergence_bisect.py reads it unmodified. Self-diff PASSES (strict and
subsequence) on the captured rich trace.

PERF NOTE (honest): this is a hot path. A ~70s boot produced ~349M records / 11GB
(~100 MB/s). The boot stays healthy (reaches D3D12 + "Superman Returns" title), so
it is usable, but the trace is large -- capture only as long as needed and analyze
by streaming (do not load the whole file into RAM). The bisector loads traces into
memory, so diff against a truncated prefix or a thread-filtered slice.

